Chapter: SubstringOverRead
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The demonstration that `substring` no longer reads past the end of its
 source. It must DIE on a UD2; if it prints a line, the bounds check is
 gone.

 Before the check, this exact program answered

   over-read = [abcde   s       PASSWORD-1234567890     ]

 which is the next allocation returned verbatim. `substring` took its start
 and length on trust and copied that many bytes, so an over-long length
 walked straight out of the source and into whatever the heap held next.

 The second text is not decoration. Bytes past a string are usually blank,
 and blank padding and a leaked neighbour look identical -- so the probe
 puts something recognisable there and the leak becomes unambiguous.

 This carries a `.fatal`, which is worth being honest about: `-Fatal` is not
 part of any routine battery, and a `.fatal` test has no `.expected` because
 the captured output is an `!EXC` dump whose RIP moves with every codegen
 change. So this file is a compile check plus a manual read. The half that a
 battery can fail on is `codex/test/ops/substring-bounds.codex`, which pins
 every legal request.

Section: Probe

  mk : Text -> Text
  mk (t) = t & ""

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let a = mk "abcde"
    in let secret = mk "PASSWORD-1234567890"
    in print-line-uni ("over-read = [" & substring a 0 40 & "]")
  end

